// THE VIRGIN OF THE WORLD OF HERMES MERCURIUS TRISMEGISTUS, TRANSLATED BY
// ANNA KINGSFORD AND EDWARD MAITLAND (LONDON: GEORGE REDWAY, 1885).
//
// The text comes from virgin-of-the-world-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/virgin-of-the-world.py from an e-text of the 1885 book, voted
// word by word against a second copy of that e-text and against the archive.org
// OCR of Google's scan of an 1885 copy; every reading the vote took and every
// correction made by hand is listed in the script, and the doubtful places
// were read on the 1885 page images. The two e-text copies descend from one
// transcription, so the scan is the only independent witness. The treatises
// are imported whole: the Korē Kosmou ("The Virgin of the World") in three
// parts, the Asclepius ("A Treatise on Initiations") in fifteen, "The
// Definitions of Asclepios" (Corpus Hermeticum XVI–XVIII) in four, the
// fragments to Tatios and to Ammon and the various fragments; with them the
// first section of Kingsford's Introduction, which is twelve pages long. The
// rest of the Introduction, Maitland's two essays and the footnotes are listed,
// not imported.
//
// WHAT THE BOOK IS. A Victorian translation, not an ancient witness. The
// translators say (p. ii) that they used Louis Ménard's French version of 1866,
// "but not entirely followed" it; the English is therefore at one remove from
// the Greek and the Latin. They founded the Hermetic Society in 1884, and the
// book was published "under the auspices of the Hermetic Society": its
// essays and notes read the Hermetic writings as an esoteric wisdom one with
// "the creed of catholic Christendom". The archive records that aim as
// context. The ancient writings are dated by the existing Hermetica records
// (Mead's Thrice-Greatest Hermes in herm.stobaeus, herm.asclepius and
// herm.corpus-hermeticum); no new date is claimed here.
//
// THE LINKS. Each part is linked to the passages of Mead's English that render
// the same ancient text, as two translations of one original
// (direct_textual_parallel, with the common source named). Neither translation
// is said to copy the other: Kingsford and Maitland worked from Ménard's French
// in 1885, Mead from the Greek and the Latin in 1906. Where the two differ in
// meaning, the difference is given in the link and in the plain words.
//
// Nothing is marked verified: the text is a careful copy, not a reading checked
// page by page against the book.

